Common Kubernetes Backoff Errors (and How to Fix Them Fast)

If you’ve spent any time with Kubernetes, you’ve probably met these infamous errors:

CrashLoopBackOff

ImagePullBackOff

ErrImagePull

CreateContainerConfigError

OOMKilled


At first, they look scary… but most have simple fixes:

🔹 CrashLoopBackOff → Pod keeps restarting. Check kubectl logs → usually missing env vars, wrong command, or probe failures.

🔹 ImagePullBackOff / ErrImagePull → Image can’t be pulled. Fix typos, tags, or add imagePullSecrets for private registries.

🔹 CreateContainerConfigError → Misconfig before start. Missing ConfigMap/Secret or bad volume. kubectl describe pod will tell you.

🔹 OOMKilled → Memory limits exceeded. Increase limits or fix memory leaks.

👉 My quick debug flow:
1️⃣ kubectl describe pod – events never lie.
2️⃣ kubectl logs --previous – see last crash.
3️⃣ kubectl top pod – check resources.

Most of the time, Kubernetes is already telling you what’s wrong — we just need to slow down and read carefully.
